\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The principal planes and lattice translations of the hcp structure, on which the nine families of \cref  {eq:families} are placed. (a)~The \emph  {basal} plane $(0001)$ (shaded, bottom) is the habit of $c_0$, $c_f$ and $c_p$; the first-order \emph  {prismatic} plane $\{10\bar  10\}$ (the narrow shaded face at the left) is the habit of $a_1,a_2,a_3$; the second-order plane $\{11\bar  20\}$ (the wide shaded region) is a diagonal plane through the cell on which \ensuremath  {\langle a\rangle }{} loops are also observed. The blue arrows are the two shortest lattice translations, $\langle a\rangle =\tfrac  13\langle 11\bar  20\rangle $ in the basal plane and $\langle c\rangle =[0001]$ along the cell axis. (b)~The inclined pyramidal-I $\{10\bar  11\}$ and pyramidal-II $\{11\bar  22\}$ planes contain $\langle c+a\rangle =\tfrac  13\langle 11\bar  23\rangle $ and carry \emph  {no} family of \cref  {eq:families}: $\langle c+a\rangle $ is the glide vector that accommodates $c$-axis strain in deformation, and the planes containing it are not observed habit planes for irradiation-induced loops. The absence of a pyramidal family is therefore an experimental statement and not a truncation. The cell is drawn at an arbitrary axial ratio; $\alpha $-Zr has $c/a\simeq 1.5944$.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hcp-cell}{{1}{5}{}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:hcp-cell@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][4][]5}{}{}{}}
