\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Reaction admissibility graph for the nine families of \cref  {eq:families}. Vertices above the datum carry positive (interstitial) polarity, those below it negative (vacancy) polarity. Blue arrows are monomer capture (P2), which acts on a vacancy loop with the opposite sign when the captured species is an interstitial; dashed orange is thermal emission (P5); the heavy red double arrow is recombination (P1); orange loops are coalescence (P3); violet is the one-way basal chain $c_0\to c_f\to c_p$ (P7) and the loop--network transfer (P8); gray is absorption at the network (P4) and the Dirichlet condition on the mobile species at a grain boundary (P9). The green edges (P10) are the channel introduced in \cref  {sec:gb-loops}, by which a loop that has grown large enough to intersect a boundary is absorbed by it --- an edge with no counterpart in a mean-field treatment, where a loop has no size relative to a boundary that is itself only a sink strength. The three prismatic variants of each character are drawn as one vertex for legibility; they are separate populations that exchange nothing with one another and differ only in habit normal.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rag}{{2}{8}{}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:rag@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][7][]8}{}{}{}}
